<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assassination of Uthman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/656">656 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The assassination of Uthman was the killing of the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uthman-ibn-affan">Uthman ibn Affan</a>, by rebels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 656 CE. Discontent with his appointments and policies had led to unrest in the provinces. His murder triggered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-fitna">First Fitna</a>, the civil war that undermined the Rashidun Caliphate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assassination-of-uthman.jpg" alt="Assassination of Uthman" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vikiçizer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayasofya_13_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Uthman, of the Umayyad clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, was elected by a consultative assembly (shura) of prominent companions of Muhammad after the assassination of Umar in 644. He relied on his Umayyad relatives, appointing kinsmen to all the provincial governorships and making grants to relatives such as Marwan ibn al-Hakam. From around 650 opinion turned against him: he was accused of nepotism, and his claim to the crown lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and his demand that provincial surplus be sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> angered the settlers of Iraq and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> the qurra under Malik ibn al-Harith expelled the governor Sa'id ibn al-As, and in January 655 discontented settlers ejected the governor Abd Allah ibn Sa'd from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, companions including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a>, Talha and Muhammad's widow A'isha criticised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>When Egyptian rebels gathered near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, Ali and Muhammad ibn Maslama persuaded them to return home with promises of redress. On the way they intercepted a courier carrying a letter, allegedly under Uthman's seal, that ordered the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to kill them. Historians now consider that it was issued by Marwan rather than Uthman. The rebels returned and laid siege to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s house, demanding his abdication. Ali, despairing of breaking Marwan's influence, withdrew, but insisted that Uthman be allowed water and sent his two sons to protect the house.</p><p>On 16 June a servant of Marwan killed with a stone Niyar ibn Iyad al-Aslami, a companion who was demanding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s abdication. The next day the rebels set the doors on fire. Uthman ordered his defenders not to fight, though Marwan and others resisted. Rebels climbed in from neighbouring houses and found him with his wife Na'ila bint al-Furafisa, reading the Qur'an. Accounts name Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kinana">Kinana</a> ibn Bishr al-Tujibi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> ibn Humran and Amr ibn al-Hamiq among his killers. The house was looted, and he was buried at night in the Jewish cemetery of Hashsh Kawkab.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Ali was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> while the rebels remained in the city, and the lack of a trial for the killers became the central grievance of Uthman's relatives. Mu'awiya, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and the companions Talha and al-Zubayr demanded retribution (qisas), and Uthman's bloodied shirt and Na'ila's severed fingers were displayed in the mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. The dispute led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-camel">Battle of the Camel</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-siffin">Battle of Siffin</a>, and raised lasting questions about rebellion and the qualifications for rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Assassination of Uthman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
